    "evidence_text": "We demonstrate the potential of our evaluation framework by evaluating existing image captioning models on a wide ranging set of synthetic datasets that we construct for diagnostic evaluation.\n\nMany existing captioning models , , ,  follow the typical encoder-decoder framework where\n\nWhile these models demonstrate promising results, quantifying image captioning performance remains a challenging problem, in a similar way to other generative tasks , .\n\nstrengths and limitations of existing models.\n\nGTD Evaluation Framework ::: Grammaticality\tAn essential criterion for an image captioning model is that the captions generated are grammatically well-formed.\n\nQuantification descriptions involve count-based and ratio-based statements, with an explicit focus on inspecting models for their counting ability.\n\nWe develop two variants for each type of dataset to enable different levels of visual complexity or specific aspects of the same reasoning type.\n\nAt test time, only the test images are available to the evaluated models.\n\nUnderlying world models are kept from the models and are used for later GTD evaluation.\n\nHowever, while BLEU reports equivalent model performance on Existential-MultiShapes and Spatial-MultiShapes, the truthfulness metric demonstrates very different results.\n\nsee that while the model performs rather well on Existential datasets, it exhibits a worse performance on Spatial data.",
